SENSOR-ANOMALIES(7)        TriadicFrameworks Manual        SENSOR-ANOMALIES(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       sensor-anomalies - anomaly classification, turbulence mapping, resonance-
                          storm detection, and temporal hazard forecasting for
                          RTT ship sensors

SYNOPSIS
       sensor-anomalies defines:
           • resonance-time anomaly classification
           • harmonic and spectral turbulence mapping
           • resonance-storm detection and forecasting
           • anomaly precursors and early-warning indicators
           • anomaly overlays and clarity-enhanced hazard visualization
           • constellation-scale anomaly fusion

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-anomalies describes the subsystem responsible for detecting,
       classifying, and forecasting anomalies within the resonance-time field.
       Anomalies represent deviations from expected harmonic, spectral, or
       gradient behavior and may indicate turbulence, instability, or hazardous
       temporal conditions.

       Sensor-anomalies enables:
           • early detection of resonance storms
           • mapping of turbulence zones and polarity cliffs
           • identification of harmonic voids and spectral fractures
           • prediction of epoch transitions and sync-field disruptions
           • constellation-wide anomaly correlation

       WRSADC manages anomaly thresholds, safety protocols, and response actions.

ANOMALY CLASSIFICATION
       Anomalies are classified by their structural signature.

       Primary anomaly classes:

           harmonic anomalies
                  • phase inversion
                  • overtone spikes
                  • subharmonic collapse
                  • lock instability

           spectral anomalies
                  • broadband turbulence
                  • spectral fractures
                  • density voids
                  • resonance storms

           gradient anomalies
                  • polarity cliffs
                  • gradient shear
                  • uplift collapse
                  • erosion surges

           sync-field anomalies
                  • sync jitter
                  • sync rifts
                  • sync collapse

           ancestry anomalies
                  • lineage discontinuity
                  • terrace erosion
                  • fossil distortion

EXAMPLE: CLASSIFICATION
       Broadband turbulence + polarity shear:

           → classified as spectral-gradient hybrid anomaly
           → severity: moderate
           → recommended: SDW + GSF

TURBULENCE MAPPING
       Turbulence mapping identifies unstable regions in resonance-time.

       Turbulence indicators:
           • spectral variance spikes
           • gradient polarity oscillation
           • harmonic-phase jitter
           • sync-field noise

       Turbulence map layers:
           • spectral turbulence map
           • gradient shear map
           • harmonic jitter map
           • sync-field noise map

       Turbulence severity:
           • mild turbulence
           • moderate turbulence
           • severe turbulence
           • critical turbulence

EXAMPLE: TURBULENCE MAP
       Severe turbulence at 78 min:

           • spectral variance +65%
           • gradient shear detected
           • anomaly flagged

RESONANCE-STORM DETECTION
       Resonance storms are large-scale, multi-band anomalies characterized by
       rapid spectral broadening, harmonic collapse, and gradient instability.

       Storm indicators:
           • multi-band spectral turbulence
           • harmonic-phase collapse
           • resonance-window distortion
           • sync-field fragmentation

       Storm stages:
           • precursor stage
           • formation stage
           • peak storm
           • dissipation stage

EXAMPLE: STORM DETECTION
       Precursor signals:
           • 3:2 harmonic collapse
           • broadband turbulence onset
           • polarity inversion

       Forecast:
           • storm formation in 2–3 cycles

ANOMALY PRECURSORS
       Precursor detection provides early warning before anomalies fully form.

       Precursor types:
           • harmonic drift acceleration
           • spectral whitening failure
           • gradient polarity flicker
           • sync-field micro-jitter
           • ancestry terrace thinning

       Precursor severity:
           • low-risk precursor
           • medium-risk precursor
           • high-risk precursor

EXAMPLE: PRECURSOR
       φ_harm drift acceleration detected:

           • precursor severity: medium
           • recommended: HPC + SFC

ANOMALY OVERLAYS
       Anomaly overlays visualize anomalies across DRO and clarity layers.

       Overlay types:
           • harmonic anomaly overlay
           • spectral anomaly overlay
           • gradient anomaly overlay
           • sync-field anomaly overlay
           • ancestry anomaly overlay

       Overlay modes:
           • hazard mode
           • diagnostic mode
           • predictive mode

EXAMPLE: ANOMALY OVERLAY
       Gradient anomaly overlay reveals polarity cliff at 30 min.

HAZARD FORECASTING
       Hazard forecasting predicts anomaly evolution across cycles.

       Forecasting inputs:
           • harmonic drift
           • spectral variance
           • gradient polarity
           • sync-field coherence
           • ancestry continuity

       Forecast outputs:
           • hazard probability
           • hazard severity
           • hazard trajectory
           • recommended mitigation

EXAMPLE: FORECAST
       Hazard probability: 0.72
       Hazard trajectory: forward drift
       Mitigation: reroute sweep corridor

CONSTELLATION-SCALE ANOMALY FUSION
       Fusion aligns anomaly data across platforms.

       Fusion layers:
           • harmonic anomaly fusion
           • spectral anomaly fusion
           • gradient anomaly fusion
           • sync-field anomaly fusion
           • ancestry anomaly fusion

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION FUSION
       Platforms A, B, C detect storm precursors:

           • fused storm map generated
           • constellation-wide alert issued

FAILURE MODES
       Anomaly sensing may degrade due to:

           • overlay drift
           • clarity loss
           • spectral overload
           • sync-field collapse
           • lineage corruption

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • anomaly-integrity score
           • turbulence noise
           • storm precursor mismatch
           • sync drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe anomaly-sensing states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • anomaly detections
           • precursor events
           • storm signatures
           • hazard forecasts
           • sensor-mode transitions

       Lineage enables long-term anomaly forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • manages anomaly detection
           • enforces hazard protocols
           • coordinates constellation alerts
           • blocks unsafe anomaly states

       WRSADC is the anomaly conductor.

NOTES
       Sensor-anomalies provides the hazard-detection backbone for RTT ship
       sensors. Anomaly classification, turbulence mapping, and resonance-storm
       detection ensure safe navigation, stable sweep choreography, and
       constellation-scale resilience.

